---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ObfuscatedEmail from '../components/ObfuscatedEmail.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import SignupBand from '../components/SignupBand.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Public Plaza"
  description="Beaumont Crossing, the public plaza at NE 44th Avenue and Fremont Street — where things stand after the 2026 summer trial and what happens next."
>

  <PageHero
    title="Public Plaza"
    subtitle="Beaumont Crossing &mdash; NE 44th Avenue at Fremont"
    image={`${base}images/hero/hero-plaza`}
    imageAlt="Neighbors at tables and umbrellas on the painted street surface at Beaumont Crossing"
    maxWidth={1200}
  />

  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <h2>Where Things Stand</h2>
      <p>
        Beaumont Crossing, the public plaza on NE 44th Avenue at Fremont, was dismantled in late
        September at the end of its three-month summer trial. The barriers and furniture are gone,
        and the street is open to traffic again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Portland Bureau of Transportation (PBOT) is now evaluating the pilot. Its online
        survey, which ran from two weeks before the closing to two weeks after, has closed, and
        the results will be part of a report PBOT will publish. That report has not been released
        yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the evaluation shows that the plaza worked well and has widespread neighborhood support,
        there would be an opportunity to reinstall Beaumont Crossing in 2027 as a full-fledged part
        of PBOT&rsquo;s Public Street Plaza Program. No decision has been made.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <h2>What Comes Next?</h2>
      <p>
        When PBOT releases its report, we&rsquo;ll post a summary and a link to the full version
        here. The summary will also appear in the January&ndash;February 2027 newsletter and in our
        monthly emailed Community Updates.
      </p>
      <p>
        From there, the Beaumont Business Association (BBA), BWNA, and PBOT will decide together
        whether to bring the plaza back.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <h2>Looking Back at the Summer</h2>
      <p>
        From its July 11 grand opening through its September 26 Summer Send-Off, Beaumont Crossing
        hosted live music, a makers market, kids&rsquo; activities, and plenty of neighbors just
        stopping by to sit in the sun. Read about the opening in
        <a href={`${base}newsletters/2026-09-beaumont-crossing-opens`}>Beaumont Crossing Opens</a>
        from the September&ndash;October newsletter, and see past plaza events on our
        <a href={`${base}events`}>events calendar</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <div class="card card--highlight plaza-contact">
        <h3>Your feedback and questions are appreciated!</h3>
        <p>
          The survey has closed, but the BWNA Plaza Committee still wants to hear from you. Email
          us with your thoughts on the plaza or any questions.
        </p>
        <ObfuscatedEmail email="plaza@bwnapdx.org" label="Email the Plaza Committee" class="btn btn-secondary" />
      </div>

      <p class="partners-note">
        Beaumont Crossing is a joint project of the Beaumont-Wilshire Neighborhood Association,
        the Beaumont Business Association (BBA), and the Portland Bureau of Transportation
        (PBOT). For more about the city's street plaza program, visit
        <a href="https://www.portland.gov/transportation/planning/plazas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PBOT's associated webpage</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SignupBand />

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .plaza-contact {
    text-align: center;
  }

  .partners-note {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xl);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--color-text-light);
  }
</style>
